>> The point I'm making here is that your perceptions of "balance" are
>> dependent solely on those voluntary restrictions.  We cannot judge a
>> game's balance by a player who isn't trying to win by all means
>> necessary -- and frankly, that's you.
>
> My view is that the game balance is *not* defined by playing "without
> restrictions"; 

There is no other way to define it.  This is standard game design; 
if you want to balance a game, you must assume that your players
will attempt to win; ie. reach the victory condition as described.

Actions that don't lead towards the victory condition are irrelevant to
the balance; deliberate refusal to employ the optimal methods of
reaching the victory condition can't be used to determine balance,
because that's now playing to an alternate victory condition: "win with
one arm tied behind my back."

If you were able to go back and design chess, you would not spend time
balancing the game based on variants such as "I'll spot you a rook", for
the same reasons.

>> I see you walk around Burdened a lot; this is tantamount to fiddling,
